WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.001
-Welcome back, everybody.

00:00:02.001 --> 00:00:03.069
I'm about to show you some books

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.503
that you should avoid
at all costs.

00:00:04.503 --> 00:00:05.805
It's time
for my latest installment

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:08.141
of my Do Not Read List.
Here we go.

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:10.676
♪ Do not read, do not read ♪

00:00:10.676 --> 00:00:14.614
♪ Do not read these books,
these books ♪

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:16.282
-And before we start,
I just want you all to know

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:19.885
that every book that I'm about
to show you is 100% real.

00:00:19.885 --> 00:00:22.922
These are actual books.
You can find them on Amazon

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:24.723
or wherever
you get your books from.

00:00:24.723 --> 00:00:25.891
These are real.

00:00:25.891 --> 00:00:27.693
Let's see what's on
my Do Not Read list.

00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:32.065
This first one up here
is a book called

00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:34.333
"Computer Programs
for the Kitchen"...

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:37.370
[ Laughter ]
by Terence F. Dicker.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.139
There's --
There's Terence Dicker.

00:00:40.139 --> 00:00:42.441
He's got his computer out
on the table

00:00:42.441 --> 00:00:44.210
next to all the food he made.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.980
You can barely tell
the computer's out there.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.250
I get -- I get hangry
just looking at it.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:52.886
Let's --
Oh, look at the back here.

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:55.888
It says,
"Exciting ways to ease

00:00:55.888 --> 00:00:59.725
kitchen and cooking chores
with a personal computer."

00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:01.160
Let's see an example.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.063
Oh, here's one right here.

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:05.498
Keeping track
of your kitchen inventory

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:08.768
by hand-typing
a 26-page computer program...

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:10.403
[ Laughter ]

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:14.507
...that has probably almost
4,000 lines of code.

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:15.741
[ Laughter ]

00:01:15.741 --> 00:01:18.244
So that will save me
a ton of time.

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:19.979
That's pretty cool.

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:22.448
Thanks.
Thanks, Chef Dicker.

00:01:22.448 --> 00:01:23.949
[ Laughter ]

00:01:23.949 --> 00:01:26.252
Next book...
[ Applause ]

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:28.488
...is called, uh...

00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:29.655
Oh, yeah.
This is a good one here.

00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:33.126
This is called
"The Educated Cat:

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:35.829
How to Teach Your Cat
to do Tricks."

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:37.463
Uh, yeah.

00:01:37.463 --> 00:01:39.299
Like apparently playing
a dueling piano

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:42.067
at an old saloon, maybe.
For cats.

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:44.470
Let's see what other tricks
you got in here.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.672
I love cat tricks, man.

00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:49.742
Oh, this one's
an interesting trick here.

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:53.413
It's two cats
kind of crammed in a stroller.

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:56.348
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.348 --> 00:01:57.817
That's interesting.

00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:00.053
Let's see what other tricks
they got here.

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:02.155
-That was the trick?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:04.857
-Yeah, that's the trick.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.857 --> 00:02:07.693
Here's another trick here.
This is a good one.

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:10.697
It's another cat in a stroller.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:13.032
They put the glasses on the cat.
-I see.

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:15.301
-Oh, man.

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:17.003
-"America's Got Talent,"
are you watching?

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:18.771
[ Laughter ]

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:21.340
Some good tricks, man.
[ Applause ]

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.975
"You tricked me, man.

00:02:22.975 --> 00:02:24.911
I thought you had a cat
in the stroller."

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:26.813
[ Laughter ]

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:29.381
"I did, actually."

00:02:29.381 --> 00:02:31.651
I thought it didn't have
20/20 vision.

00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:33.519
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:35.688
He tricked you again.

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:38.591
He's perfectly fine.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:40.693
Good. Good stuff.
Let's check out another one.

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:43.128
This one's a children's book.

00:02:43.128 --> 00:02:44.964
It's called "Mac's Choice:

00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:46.866
A Story About Choices
and Drug Use."

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:51.370
There's a caterpillar and a
cockroach on the cover there.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.974
This is cool.
I saw this earlier.

00:02:54.974 --> 00:02:57.443
It's signed by the author,

00:02:57.443 --> 00:03:01.447
and gifted to a kid
by an aluminum company.

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:03.916
[ Laughter ]

00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:05.250
Makes sense.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:10.857
Let's just go to the end here,
see what happens here.

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:13.960
It says, "Mac was all alone now.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.929
He started to shake badly as his
body craved more cocaine."

00:03:16.929 --> 00:03:19.398
[ Laughter ]
-Wait. What?

00:03:19.398 --> 00:03:22.902
-"Anyway, good night, kids."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:25.270
Turn the lights off.

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:26.805
[ Laughter continues ]

00:03:26.805 --> 00:03:30.275
-Wait.
-Have a good night's sleep.

00:03:30.275 --> 00:03:33.178
Next is a young adult novel.
They're always popular.

00:03:33.178 --> 00:03:37.416
They're always having helpful
lessons about growing up, life.

00:03:37.416 --> 00:03:38.650
This one should help, as well.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:42.255
This one is called
"Mom is Dating Weird Wayne."

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:44.924
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:47.026
Look, we've all been there.
Mom is looking for love,

00:03:47.026 --> 00:03:48.961
hits the dating scene,
and suddenly a dude

00:03:48.961 --> 00:03:51.197
dressed up as Dracula
starts coming over for dinner.

00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:53.432
[ Laughter ]

00:03:53.432 --> 00:03:55.702
"If you vant to,
you can call me dad."

00:03:55.702 --> 00:03:57.636
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.636 --> 00:04:01.373
"We're not there yet.

00:04:01.373 --> 00:04:02.842
We're not there yet, dad.

00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:04.744
So I'm gonna call you Vlad."

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:07.346
[ Laughter ]

00:04:07.346 --> 00:04:10.315
We're down to our last book,
guys, unfortunately.

00:04:10.315 --> 00:04:12.218
This is a -- Please.
-Oh.

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:14.053
-Come on, man.
-Anyway, look,

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:15.154
there's other things
we have to get to.

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:16.989
There's interviews,
there's a show.

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:18.591
You know, it's a busy week,
you know?

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:20.326
-Okay.
So we're down to our last book.

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:21.827
It's a how-to book.

00:04:21.827 --> 00:04:26.532
It's called "Two-Hour Vests,"
by Mary Jo Hiney.

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:28.434
[ Laughter ]

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:30.603
Vests I can make in two hours?

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:32.105
Now we're talking.

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:35.707
Let's see what we can make here.

00:04:35.707 --> 00:04:37.843
There's a lot of them.

00:04:37.843 --> 00:04:39.645
We have the "Young at Heart,"
the "Now and Forever,"

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:42.882
"Moments in Time,"
"Breath of Fresh Air."

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:43.983
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:45.418
Let's see that one.

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:48.587
"Breath of Fresh Air,"
which is nice and breezy there.

00:04:48.587 --> 00:04:51.324
Ooh, yeah, look at this.

00:04:51.324 --> 00:04:52.958
Yeah, that makes sense.
Because when I think of

00:04:52.958 --> 00:04:55.094
"fresh air" I think
of the color brown.

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:56.996
[ Laughter ]

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:59.465
Kamal, can I get some
"fresh air" music, please?

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:01.067
♪♪

00:05:01.067 --> 00:05:03.302
Let's read this right here.

00:05:03.302 --> 00:05:05.771
"Breath of fresh air.

00:05:05.771 --> 00:05:07.606
Small talk smothers you.

00:05:07.606 --> 00:05:09.509
You prefer quiet company.

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:12.611
Soft and simple contemplation
by the fire.

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:14.747
To all who love you,

00:05:14.747 --> 00:05:18.484
your serenity and ease
are a breath of fresh air."

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:20.119
Yeah.
"Your vest speaks softly

00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:21.853
of the comfort that you desire."

00:05:21.853 --> 00:05:23.255
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:23.255 --> 00:05:26.725
So by wearing it,
you're the breath of fresh air.

00:05:26.725 --> 00:05:28.227
-Ah, yeah.
-You get it now?

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:29.728
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:29.728 --> 00:05:32.364
-That's all the time we have
for "Do Not Read."

00:05:32.364 --> 00:05:33.832
If you have a book
that you think should be on

00:05:33.832 --> 00:05:35.701
our next Do Not Read list,
send your titles to

00:05:35.701 --> 00:05:37.369 line:0%
donotread@tonightshow.com.

00:05:37.369 --> 00:05:39.939 line:0%
Stick around. We'll be right
back with Shailene Woodley.

00:05:39.939 --> 00:05:41.107 line:0%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:44.711 line:0%
♪♪

00:05:44.711 --> 00:05:47.747 line:0%
-Took two hours.
Two hours -- that's it.

00:05:47.747 --> 00:05:56.089 line:0%
♪♪

